#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:5
#: 37a8c3e491a5470881618d9fcb8de288
msgid "Advanced Failsafe Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:7
#: 2e7f94ef47d448a3b70754a2e86f3f93
msgid "The core failsafe functionality of Plane is based on RTL (Return to Launch). It is able to initiate a RTL if the aircraft loses contact with the ground station or loses RC control. That is fine for most users, but in some situations more advanced failsafe capabilities are needed. That is what the Advanced Failsafe options are for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:14
#: 49edcae4812a4b4f8019f72777ed7b46
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:16
#: 7514eb1409f7459082019d6ebb672ca4
msgid "The advanced failsafe options for Plane were added for the `Outback Challenge <http://www.uavoutbackchallenge.com.au/>`__ competition, and the features are designed to fit the rules of that competition. Whereas normal failsafe is oriented around saving the aircraft in case control is lost by the pilot, the OBC rules are oriented around ensuring safe operation in a defined region of airspace. This means that the advanced failsafe options are designed to deliberately crash the aircraft if there is any risk that it may fly outside of the region of airspace defined by a geographic boundary and a maximum altitude."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:25
#: 54b52e9e56834fbeb9adf89b5e675ce5
msgid "While the advanced failsafe features of Plane were designed for the OBC competition, they can be useful in other situations, and are very flexible. They are also harder to configure, so please read this page carefully several times before deciding whether to enable these features on your aircraft."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:32
#: c0e9af05d1524c40b9efd5eebe5183f6
msgid "Mission based"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:34
#: f956883ed7be4f928f119a6ad7ea57c9
msgid "The key difference between normal failsafe and the advanced failsafe (AFS) options is that the AFS options are mission based. When a failsafe option (such as loss of GPS lock or loss of ground station communications link) happens, the AFS options specify a waypoint number in the mission that the aircraft will switch to. This allows the pilot to configure a complex series of actions to take when a failsafe event occurs - anything that can be scripted as an Plane mission can be made to happen on failsafe events."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:43
#: d3916eff1c1444bab7379e27f03b453b
msgid "Typically the pilot will setup the AFS options for failsafe conditions so that the aircraft will loiter at its current location for some period of time (say one minute) and then proceed back towards home via a pre-determined flight path. It may also include changes in airspeed, changes in altitude, automatic landing or anything else that can be programmed in a mission. If the failsafe event stops (for example GPS lock is regained) then the aircraft will switch back to the mission item it was previously flying towards and continue the mission."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:52
#: e42a466c16124d66b1e7f3e75c371883
msgid "This makes the AFS options only really appropriate for AUTO missions. If you are primarily flying Plane in CRUISE mode or other modes then you should use the standard failsafe options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:57
#: dedc02517387400f88e8fce587692036
msgid "Enabling the AFS failsafe system"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:59
#: f415a8e5c5844d879f5d67560d7b600b
msgid "To enable the AFS failsafe system you need to set the :ref:`AFS_ENABLE<AFS_ENABLE>` parameter to 1. The default is zero, which means all the other options are disabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:61
#: c91d7324b61747cabced3c6a7f0e2ca4
msgid "Note that the AFS system is only built into Plane by default only on higher memory autopilot boards like the CubeOrange and Pixhawk6X. Check the :ref:`Firmware Limitations page <binary-features>` for your autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:65
#: d7009fa80a36423ab0358c3d320b9a60
msgid "AFS Termination"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:67
#: e4b4c8c013224703be5870f5e8215839
msgid "The concept of \"flight termination\" is key to understanding the AFS failsafe system. Termination is where the aircraft deliberately dives into the ground by setting all control surfaces to maximum and throttle is disarmed so as to enter a spin, or in the case of QuadPlane, there is also the option to immediately QLAND instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:72
#: 1f551cf34b024f2aab330057e762d962
msgid "The AFS system will only start a termination if the :ref:`AFS_TERM_ACTION<AFS_TERM_ACTION>` is set to the magic value 42 or 43. For any other value the AFS system will print a message on the GCS console saying that it wants to terminate, but won't actually change the control surfaces at all. Using a value other than 42 or 43 is useful for test flights where you don't want the aircraft to terminate on a failsafe event. A value of 42 immediately crashed the plane, while, if a QuadPlane, a value of 43 will immediately execute a QLAND."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:79
#: 0d27717e9f854a97b735dafe4737f36b
msgid "Note that if :ref:`AFS_TERM_ACTION<AFS_TERM_ACTION>` is not set to 42 then other normal failsafe code is still active, for example if you have a geofence enabled then the aircraft will fly back to the geofence return point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:83
#: 72be6398a06b4604a06bf779d3ae3cad
msgid "When enabled, the AFS termination system also sets up the secondary IO micro-controller on the autopilot to terminate the aircraft if communication is interrupted between the main FMU micro-controller and the IO micro-controller, for example if the flight firmware crashes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:88
#: 3d2ba6b4079d46d99d9fb10d63a65af1
msgid "An AFS flight termination is not recoverable. Once your aircraft starts a termination, there is no way to recover."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:92
#: 8a6d7bb7c80e4e3c8aaf3146738504d4
msgid "Types of Failsafe Events"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:94
#: 2df452e3ed244ed8b4adc40962eb2b44
msgid "The AFS failsafe system supports five of types of failsafe events:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:96
#: c28026d5976e4957b1f8068817e47cc4
msgid "geofence breach"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:97
#: 226c7d97c0524d34b936a266aed6552f
msgid "maximum pressure altitude breach"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:98
#: 98c91ecf27794343a3eeae2d54f7effd
msgid "GPS loss"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:99
#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:193
#: fa93b1d37d8d4573bc245d2c95d56740
#: 895ec3b5aa1e4d76977b9ffdb75f2605
msgid "Ground station communications loss"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:100
#: 62322d711d014e5c8efdad9c392cdd1b
msgid "barometer failure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:102
#: 8277f4ab1b084b8a9c08027af75718ae
msgid "Each of these types of failures has its own specific handling, which is described below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:106
#: 3407aee766d248e3b665c4c682b42280
msgid "Geofence Breach"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:108
#: 728a97efb1fc4c389fef1ed828d2ab10
msgid "If a :ref:`geofence <geofencing>` is enabled then the AFS failsafe module will monitor the aircraft for a breach of the boundaries of the geofence (and lower and upper geofence altitudes if set). If a breach happens then the AFS system will immediately terminate the flight (see termination above)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:114
#: 00a9d7643a1f4faf89229e3ae692f206
msgid "In addition, if the total straight-line distance from arming point exceeds the :ref:`AFS_MAX_RANGE<AFS_MAX_RANGE>` distance, then termination will occur."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:117
#: 100d7d53abc54b638d5244032aef868e
msgid "Maximum pressure altitude breach"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:119
#: fc27ecdaa34143b5bba77b81264522c6
msgid "When sharing airspace with other aircraft it is usual practice to define the available flight altitudes in terms of a common reference pressure, typically QNH (a reference to \"nautical height\"). The QNH reference pressure, measured in millibar, is distributed to all aircraft either via a radio message or through aviation internet and weather sites."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:125
#: 37d01d9616334850be5ff34a330c5476
msgid "Aircraft then use their barometer to measure the pressure relative to that QNH pressure, which gives them an altitude reference which all aircraft in the area should be using."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:129
#: 41e299e97d7a4ebcbfc231def04fea99
msgid "The AFS system is able to enforce a pressure altitude limit by setting the QNH pressure in the :ref:`AFS_QNH_PRESSURE<AFS_QNH_PRESSURE>` parameter, as a value in millibars. The pilot should then also set a pressure altitude limit using the :ref:`AFS_AMSL_LIMIT<AFS_AMSL_LIMIT>` parameter (in meters). Note that this pressure altitude limit is relative to sea level (AMSL stands for \"above mean sea level\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:136
#: 4fb1471b45f845edbae24ae7148a96db
msgid "If both of these parameters are set then the AFS system fill monitor pressure altitude and will initiate a termination if the pressure altitude rises above the :ref:`AFS_AMSL_LIMIT<AFS_AMSL_LIMIT>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:140
#: f637e535a22d41f9b6ada8cb51294fc5
msgid "You need to be very careful to set the right :ref:`AFS_QNH_PRESSURE<AFS_QNH_PRESSURE>` for your local conditions on the day of your flight, as the QNH pressure can be very different on different days."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:144
#: ecb96d452b074605b1b1506b09574a3f
msgid "In addition to the QNH pressure limit, the AFS system also monitors the health of your barometer. If the barometer is unhealthy for 5 seconds then the AFS system will check the :ref:`AFS_AMSL_ERR_GPS<AFS_AMSL_ERR_GPS>` parameter. If it is -1 (the default) then the aircraft will terminate immediately. If it is not -1 then the AFS system will use the :ref:`AFS_AMSL_ERR_GPS<AFS_AMSL_ERR_GPS>` value as a margin to add to the GPS height, and will allow the flight to continue if the GPS altitude plus the :ref:`AFS_AMSL_ERR_GPS<AFS_AMSL_ERR_GPS>` value (in meters) is below the :ref:`AFS_AMSL_LIMIT<AFS_AMSL_LIMIT>` value. The purpose of this margin is to account for the inaccuracy of GPS altitudes. A value of 200 is reasonable for safety to ensure the :ref:`AFS_AMSL_LIMIT<AFS_AMSL_LIMIT>` pressure altitude is not breached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:157
#: 9fc3a492fa4c41bab5894988a9caf3ca
msgid "GPS Loss"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:159
#: 2c3e430218384a5491ac99b973e9f250
msgid "The AFS system monitors the health of your GPS receivers throughout the flight. If all of your available GPS receivers lose position lock then this initiates a GPS failure failsafe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:163
#: 00ccad5bc4a14949951d9ce3087f5110
msgid "When a GPS failure occurs (which is defined as loss of GPS lock for 3 seconds) the AFS system will look at the :ref:`AFS_WP_GPS_LOSS<AFS_WP_GPS_LOSS>` parameter. This parameter species a waypoint number in your mission to use when a GPS failure occurs. If :ref:`AFS_WP_GPS_LOSS<AFS_WP_GPS_LOSS>` is non-zero the aircraft will change current waypoint to the waypoint number specified in :ref:`AFS_WP_GPS_LOSS<AFS_WP_GPS_LOSS>`. You should setup your mission so that the aircraft will perform whatever actions you want on GPS loss. For example, you could have a set of waypoints starting at number 10 which first loiter on the spot for 30 seconds, and then proceed back to the airfield. You would then set :ref:`AFS_WP_GPS_LOSS<AFS_WP_GPS_LOSS>` to 10 to enable that part of the mission on loss of GPS lock."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:174
#: aaa9a8ac488548ccb65cb435741b9beb
msgid "When setting up mission items for GPS lock it is sometimes useful to include \"loiter at the current location\" waypoints. That is achieved by setting both the latitude and longitude of LOITER mission commands to zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:179
#: adda64bd709b42e4aae753fde0bdb9de
msgid "If the GPS recovers after a GPS failsafe has started, then the aircraft will automatically resume its mission where it left off."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:182
#: 6d0cb2dffe0d49298587dc41d20e3708
msgid "If :ref:`AFS_MAX_GPS_LOSS<AFS_MAX_GPS_LOSS>` is set to a non-zero number, then it is used as a maximum count of the number of GPS failures that will be allowed while returning to the mission after GPS lock is re-established. This counter is only incremented if the 2nd GPS failure happens at least 30 seconds after the previous one (to account for a short period of GPS failure)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:188
#: 6d758f71d35641fbaacbbf226202859c
msgid "If during a period of GPS loss the aircraft also loses communications with the ground station then this is termed a \"dual loss\", and the aircraft will terminate if :ref:`AFS_DUAL_LOSS<AFS_DUAL_LOSS>` is 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:195
#: 62f4cdd4f7f44286bc262dd8aa12c471
msgid "The AFS system monitors the health of the link between your ground station and your aircraft. It does this by looking for HEARTBEAT MAVLink messages coming from the ground station."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:199
#: 96e6ae23abeb41218368ce940e843f45
msgid "If the aircraft does not receive a HEARTBEAT message for a period of AFS_GCS_TIMEOUT seconds then it enters a GCS failsafe state. (The default is 10 seconds.) It then looks for a :ref:`AFS_WP_COMMS<AFS_WP_COMMS>` parameter, and if that is non-zero it will change the current target waypoint to the one given in :ref:`AFS_WP_COMMS<AFS_WP_COMMS>`. You should set up a section of your mission with whatever actions you want to take on loss of communications. This jump to a designated mission item normally only occur if the failsafe happens while in AUTO mode. To make it occur in any autothrottle controlled mode ( like CRUISE or GUIDED), you will need to set the :ref:`AFS_OPTIONS<AFS_OPTIONS>` bit 1 (+2 to value of param)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:206
#: ea894a2fbd0144a9b9165fb1b4e8ffb3
msgid "If GPS lock is lost at the same time as GCS communications is lost then that is considered a \"dual loss\", and the aircraft will immediately terminate if the :ref:`AFS_DUAL_LOSS<AFS_DUAL_LOSS>` is enabled (\"1\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:210
#: ea4a3553d4db4b9fb39c60d4bab8cdca
msgid "Note that the monitoring of HEARTBEAT messages only tells the autopilot that it can see messages from the ground station. It does not mean the ground station can see messages from the aircraft. So it is quite possible for your ground station to be reporting loss of communication while the aircraft is still receiving HEARTBEAT messages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:216
#: d48263856ae648b787971ec96e3b0ae8
msgid "If :ref:`AFS_MAX_COM_LOSS<AFS_MAX_COM_LOSS>` is set to a non-zero number, then it is used as a maximum count of the number of communication failures that will be allowed while returning to the mission after communications is re-established. This counter is only incremented if the 2nd communication failure happens at least 30 seconds after the previous one (to account for a short period of communications failure)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:223
#: 71b994c3090c4b9daf79660832dcfa76
msgid "If the GSC loss is recovered while in this failsafe state, then, normally, the mission item pointer is returned to whatever mission item was active when the failsafe occurred. To prevent this, and continue on with whatever missions sequence is executing due to the failsafe, you will need to set the :ref:`AFS_OPTIONS<AFS_OPTIONS>` bit 0 (+1 to the value)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:225
#: f8abc35eb20440d9acf0efe4983bae8c
msgid "even if the GSC AFS failsafe was entered from a mode other than AUTO, and the :ref:`AFS_OPTIONS<AFS_OPTIONS>` bit 0 is set, the vehicle will start executing the current item pointed to when the failsafe was entered, even though it was not in AUTO mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:228
#: 1ac3945fddd641fcabe96b498ae06346
msgid "RC Loss"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:230
#: 9c2f6a2f827c4363a099c5bdb532496f
msgid "If RC control is lost in for more than :ref:`AFS_RC_FAIL_TIME<AFS_RC_FAIL_TIME>` milliseconds, flight termination is activated. This termination mode is only enabled if :ref:`AFS_RC_FAIL_TIME<AFS_RC_FAIL_TIME>` is non-zero and :ref:`AFS_ENABLE<AFS_ENABLE>` is 1. For the OBC rules it should be set to 1500 (giving 1.5 seconds)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:233
#: 543eb6dd83864f51b9375e38c2b4f730
msgid "If :ref:`AFS_RC_MAN_ONLY<AFS_RC_MAN_ONLY>` is set to 1 then this will only occur if in a manual mode. Otherwise, it will occur in any flight mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:236
#: a2eb52c6f7d5431580972795b068d2ee
msgid "Monitoring the AFS system"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:238
#: 44f63bb93077426782bb8b4e4d28988b
msgid "The AFS system provides some additional parameters to make it easier to monitor the health of the failsafe system using external electronics (such as an external failsafe board)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:242
#: 88caa1affb414c74939df5b36ec10d1f
msgid "The key parameters are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:244
#: 41f9f29883434e2eb2a96ec6c8b0f931
msgid ":ref:`AFS_TERM_PIN<AFS_TERM_PIN>`: This is a digital pin which is set to a high voltage if termination is started. Note that this pin will go high on termination even if the :ref:`AFS_TERM_ACTION<AFS_TERM_ACTION>` parameter is not set to 42."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:247
#: f9f5879888d84ea689009e35fcab0e60
msgid ":ref:`AFS_HB_PIN<AFS_HB_PIN>`: This is a digital pin number for a pin which is toggled at a rate of 10Hz by the failsafe system. If termination occurs and a :ref:`AFS_TERM_PIN<AFS_TERM_PIN>` value is not set then the heartbeat pin will stop toggling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:251
#: 40b4e0fb85d24d1a82b49f68c9179455
msgid ":ref:`AFS_MAN_PIN<AFS_MAN_PIN>`: This is a digital pin number for a pin which goes high when the aircraft is in MANUAL mode. It may be useful with some external failsafe boards to detect manual mode and behave differently."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:257
#: 6efff95af45043adaaac19db349f8525
msgid "Manual Termination"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:259
#: bfc025bdf29d4dfd8a2db7c6b4d33aee
msgid "Apart from automatic termination it is also important for the aircraft's operator to be able to terminate the aircraft immediately if they think the aircraft is a danger to people or other aircraft. To force an immediate termination you should use the :ref:`AFS_TERMINATE<AFS_TERMINATE>` parameter. By setting that parameter to 1 the aircraft will immediately terminate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:266
#: 0f5beb80cecb43fea95fa5f58b50f08b
msgid "AFS_OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:267
#: 6ba20c2e3b8848dcbb8aa853084cade7
msgid "The behavior of AFS due to GSC loss can be modified by the setting of the :ref:`AFS_OPTIONS<AFS_OPTIONS>` bitmask parameter (no bits are set by default):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:269
#: 6cc390bedf5a45368de5dd11aac381c4
msgid "bit 0 (+1), if set, the aircraft will continue with the current mission item even after GCS connection is recovered. If not set, the aircraft will jump back to the mission item right before GCS failsafe occurs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:270
#: cc530381040e480583dfc142457d3e06
msgid "bit 1 (+2), if set, will also force entering AUTO when GCS failsafe occurs while in any throtlled controlled modes other than AUTO (ie CRUISE, GUIDED, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:271
#: bb42f509d2a24e9d8b1f124e3703ed57
msgid "bit 2 (+4), if set and the vehicle is already in return path (the current mission item is already ahead of DO_RETURN_PATH_START mission item), then the aircraft will not take action when GCS failsafe occurs. For example, if GCS failsafe occurs during a landing attempt, the aircraft should just land instead of starting the landing sequence again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:274
#: 9f33b239a4f74d5cb243400d70919587
msgid "Example AFS failsafe mission"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:276
#: b1da21b5e6b947a8b54f6bfb2979ce22
msgid "Setting up a AFS failsafe mission takes time, and needs to be done very carefully. To help you understand what is possible you may find the following example files useful"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:280
#: 9f7b7e054de64973aeeb9d159abb29dc
msgid "A `waypoint mission <https://github.com/tridge/cuav/blob/master/cuav/data/way.txt>`__ for the 2014 Outback Challenge with waypoints for different AFS failures commented in the file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:283
#: a6c465e4cffc42a28df7ae773b1f17e7
msgid "A `geofence file <https://github.com/tridge/cuav/blob/master/cuav/data/fence.txt>`__ for the 2014 Outback Challenge"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:287
#: a853970064fe4b8aa43f120f19faa719
msgid "Testing the AFS system in SITL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:289
#: 79e98a99bcd84761aa1d9d8010eae1ab
msgid "It is highly recommended that you extensively test the AFS system using the :ref:`SITL simulation system <dev:simulation-2>` before using it on a real aircraft. You can simulate all types of in-flight failures using the SIM\\_ parameters. To start SITL in Kingaroy ready for OBC testing you would use:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:299
#: 3d860cd2c25c4a8283e33df4f092cbdb
msgid "The key parameters for failsafe testing in SITL are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:301
#: da97a872c98e4525813ba46523c37491
msgid "Test GPS failure: param set SIM_GPS_DISABLE 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:302
#: 2a116dda9d6e4d3c93f7130eb21e585d
msgid "Test RC failure: param set SIM_RC_FAIL 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:303
#: ae1292ad2047434a9af8dc14b37a67d9
msgid "Test comms failure: set heartbeat 0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:304
#: 3cad4bde87344956bc1630827280c737
msgid "Test fence failure: switch to CRUISE mode and fly across boundary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:305
#: e9a75026204d42f3a0ecd68303ed1590
msgid "Test QNH failure: param set :ref:`AFS_AMSL_LIMIT<AFS_AMSL_LIMIT>` = 100"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:308
#: b856b79f5b044dbdbccdf9663dba2263
msgid "Additional tips for AFS failsafe users"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/advanced-failsafe-configuration.rst:310
#: 3e159531515243ec928ba9d5c54aa751
